-- IPv6 explanations rewritten in Jeremy's IT Lab style (Day 31, 32, 33).
-- Short, clear, open, one idea per line, not congested. Question statements, options and correct answers UNCHANGED.
-- 133 questions: Anki-NF-1342 to Anki-NF-1469 (128) + Anki-IPC-3054, Anki-IPC-3085, Anki-IPS-4103, Anki-IPS-4104, Anki-NA-2021.
-- Two factual fixes: FD is NOT 'Feasible Distance' (NF-1372, NF-1376); extra 6th-card header reference tables removed (NF-1425, NF-1426, NF-1427, NF-1428, NF-1429, NF-1430, NF-1431).
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: RIP Versions</div><p>RIPng stands for RIP Next Generation. It is the version of RIP made for IPv6. It works just like RIPv2, but it carries IPv6 prefixes instead of IPv4 networks.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option D</span> <strong>RIPng (RIP Next Generation)</strong>: RIPng is the IPv6 version of RIP. The ng means Next Generation.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>RIPv2</strong>: RIPv2 is the IPv4 version. It cannot carry IPv6 routes.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>RIPv3</strong>: There is no RIPv3. The IPv6 version is called RIPng.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>OSPFv3</strong>: OSPFv3 is the IPv6 version of OSPF, not a version of RIP.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>ng = Next Generation = IPv6. RIPng is for RIP, OSPFv3 is for OSPF.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Connectivity (CCNA / Day 25 Flashcards (1)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPC-3054';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
  <div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: OSPF Versions</div><p>OSPFv3 is the version of OSPF made for IPv6.</p><p>It works like OSPFv2, but it carries IPv6 prefixes and uses IPv6 link-local addresses for neighbor adjacencies.</p></div>
  <div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option B</span> <strong>OSPFv3</strong>: OSPFv3 is the version of OSPF used for IPv6.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>OSPFv4</strong>: There is no OSPFv4.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>OSPFv1</strong>: There is no OSPFv1 in use.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>OSPFv2</strong>: OSPFv2 is the version used for IPv4, not IPv6.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>v2 = IPv4, v3 = IPv6. If the question says IPv6 and OSPF, the answer is always OSPFv3.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Connectivity (CCNA / Day 26 Flashcards - OSPF (Part 1)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPC-3085';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: DNS Record Types</div><p>DNS maps names to addresses, and each record type maps a name to a different kind of data. The AAAA record is the one that maps a hostname to an IPv6 address.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option B</span> <strong>AAAA</strong>: AAAA records map a hostname to an IPv6 address. That is exactly what the question asks for.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>A</strong>: A records map names to IPv4 addresses, not IPv6.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>MX</strong>: MX records point to the mail server for a domain, not to a host address.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>CNAME</strong>: CNAME records map one name to another name, an alias. They do not map a name to an IP address.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>AAAA = IPv6. A = IPv4. Learn the pair together.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Services (CCNA / Day 38 Flashcards - DNS)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPS-4103';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: DNS AAAA Records</div><p>DNS maps a name to an address. An AAAA record holds an IPv6 address. An A record holds an IPv4 address.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option C</span> <strong>IPv6</strong>: AAAA records hold IPv6 addresses, so the blank is IPv6.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>multicast</strong>: Multicast is a type of address, not what an AAAA record holds. AAAA records hold ordinary unicast host IPv6 addresses.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>MAC</strong>: A MAC address is a Layer 2 hardware address. It is never stored in a DNS record.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>IPv4</strong>: IPv4 addresses are held in A records, not AAAA records.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>AAAA record = IPv6 address. A record = IPv4 address.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - IP Services (CCNA / Day 38 Flashcards - DNS)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-IPS-4104';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: Ethernet EtherType Values</div><p>The Type field in an Ethernet frame holds an EtherType value. It tells the receiver which protocol is carried inside the frame. Each common protocol has its own well-known hex value.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option A</span> <strong>0x86DD</strong>: The Ethernet Type value for IPv6 is 0x86DD.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>0x0800</strong>: 0x0800 is the EtherType for IPv4, not IPv6.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>0x8100</strong>: 0x8100 is the EtherType that marks an 802.1Q VLAN tag, not IPv6.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>0x0806</strong>: 0x0806 is the EtherType for ARP, not IPv6.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Memorize the four: IPv4 0x0800, IPv6 0x86DD, ARP 0x0806, VLAN 0x8100.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Access (CCNA / Day 05 Flashcards - Ethernet LAN Switching (Part 1)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NA-2021';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
  <div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Address Length</div><p>An IPv6 address is 128 bits long.</p><p>That is four times the 32 bits of an IPv4 address.</p><p>It is written as 8 groups of 16 bits, and each group is four hex digits.</p><p>Every extra bit doubles the number of addresses, so IPv6 has about 340 undecillion addresses. We will never run out.</p></div>
  <div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option A</span> <strong>128</strong>: An IPv6 address is 128 bits long.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>64</strong>: 64 bits is only the interface identifier half of an IPv6 address, not the whole address.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>256</strong>: No IP address is 256 bits long. IPv6 is 128 bits.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>32</strong>: 32 bits is the length of an IPv4 address, not IPv6.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>IPv4 = 32 bits, IPv6 = 128 bits. Four times the bits, but far more than four times the addresses.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 31 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 1)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1342';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Hexadecimal Quartets</div><p>An IPv6 address has 8 quartets, separated by colons.</p><ul><li>Each quartet is 4 hex digits, which is 16 bits.</li><li>8 quartets x 16 bits = 128 bits, the full IPv6 address.</li><li>Example: 2001:0db8:0000:0000:0000:ff00:0042:8329 has 8 groups.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option B</span> <strong>8</strong>: An IPv6 address has 8 quartets. Each one is four hex digits.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>4</strong>: 4 is the number of octets in an IPv4 address, not the number of quartets in IPv6.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>16</strong>: 16 is the number of bits in one quartet, not the number of quartets.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>6</strong>: There is no 6-quartet IPv6 address. It is always 8 quartets.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>8 quartets of 16 bits = 128 bits. Eight groups, four hex digits each.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 31 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 1)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1343';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: Shortening IPv6 Addresses</div><p>You can shorten an IPv6 address in two ways. One way is to remove the leading 0s from each quartet. The value of the quartet does not change.</p><ul><li>0db8 becomes db8</li><li>0042 becomes 42</li><li>0000 becomes 0</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option B</span> <strong>leading 0s</strong>: You remove the leading 0s from each quartet. 0db8 shortens to db8 because the leading zero adds nothing to the value.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>all 0s</strong>: You cannot remove all the 0s from a quartet. A quartet of all zeros shortens to a single 0, not to nothing.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>leading 1s</strong>: There is no rule about removing leading 1s. Only leading 0s may be dropped.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>trailing 0s</strong>: Trailing 0s cannot be removed. Removing them would change the value. 4200 is not the same as 42.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Leading zeros are the only thing you can drop from a quartet. 00ab becomes ab, 0000 becomes 0.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 31 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 1)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1344';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: Shortening IPv6 Addresses - Double Colon</div><p>You can shorten an IPv6 address by replacing a run of all-zero quartets with a double colon (::). You can only do this once in an address. If you use it twice, no one can tell how many zero quartets each :: stands for.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option B</span> <strong>double colon (::) *can only do this once per address</strong>: A run of all-zero quartets is replaced with a double colon (::), and this can only be done once per address. Using it twice would make the address ambiguous.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>double colon (::) *can be used as many times as you like*</strong>: The double colon can only be used once per address. Using it twice makes the address ambiguous.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>single colon (:)</strong>: A single colon is just the normal separator between quartets. It does not replace anything.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>zero (0)</strong>: A single zero is what one all-zero quartet shortens to. A run of zero quartets is replaced by ::, not by 0.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Double colon once only. If you see :: twice in an address, the address is invalid.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 31 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 1)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1345';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
  <div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Enterprise Allocation</div><p>An enterprise normally receives a /48 block from its ISP.</p><p>A /48 leaves 16 bits for subnets, so the enterprise can make over 65,000 subnets.</p><p>Each subnet then uses a /64, with 64 bits left for hosts.</p></div>
  <div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option C</span> <strong>/48</strong>: An enterprise normally receives a /48 block.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>/32</strong>: A /32 is a bigger block, given to an ISP, not to one enterprise.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>/56</strong>: A /56 is a smaller block, often given to a small site or a home, not to an enterprise.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>/64</strong>: A /64 is the prefix for one single subnet, not the block for a whole enterprise.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>ISP /32, enterprise /48, subnet /64.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 31 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 1)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1346';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Subnet Prefix Length</div><p>IPv6 subnets normally use a /64 prefix.</p><ul><li>A /64 leaves 64 bits for the interface ID.</li><li>That 64-bit host part is what SLAAC and EUI-64 need to build an address.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option D</span> <strong>/64</strong>: /64 is the normal IPv6 subnet size. The other 64 bits make the interface ID, which is exactly the size EUI-64 makes.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>/32</strong>: A /32 is a large block given to an ISP, not a subnet.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>/48</strong>: A /48 is the block an enterprise gets from its ISP, not one subnet.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>/128</strong>: A /128 is one single host address, like a host route. It is not a subnet.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Subnet = /64. If the question asks for the normal IPv6 subnet size, the answer is /64.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 31 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 1)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1347';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: Enabling IPv6 Routing</div><p>IPv6 routing is OFF by default on a router. IPv4 routing is on by default. Without this command, the router can send and receive IPv6, but it will NOT forward IPv6 traffic between networks.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option B</span> <strong>ipv6 unicast-routing</strong>: The command is ipv6 unicast-routing, entered in global configuration mode. It turns the router into an IPv6 router.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>ipv6 routing</strong>: There is no command called ipv6 routing. The correct command is ipv6 unicast-routing.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>ip routing</strong>: ip routing enables IPv4 routing, not IPv6 routing.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>ipv6 enable</strong>: ipv6 enable turns IPv6 on for a single interface. It does not make the router forward IPv6 packets between interfaces.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>IPv6 routing on a router = ipv6 unicast-routing in global config. Just like ip routing does for IPv4.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 31 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 1)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1348';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: Configuring an IPv6 Address</div><p>To put an IPv6 address on an interface, use the ipv6 address command in interface configuration mode. IPv6 uses a prefix length after a slash. There are no subnet masks in IPv6.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option C</span> <strong>ipv6 address address/prefix-length</strong>: The command is ipv6 address address/prefix-length, for example ipv6 address 2001:db8::1/64. The prefix length follows the address after a slash.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>ipv6 unicast-routing</strong>: ipv6 unicast-routing turns on IPv6 routing globally. It does not assign an address to an interface.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>ipv6 address address subnet-mask</strong>: IPv6 does not use subnet masks. The address is followed by a prefix length, such as /64.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>ip address address subnet-mask</strong>: That is the IPv4 command. IPv6 uses ipv6 address with a prefix length, not a subnet mask.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>IPv6 addressing: ipv6 address, then the address, then /prefix-length. No subnet mask anywhere in IPv6.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 31 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 1)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1349';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
  <div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: EUI - Extended Unique Identifier</div><p>EUI stands for Extended Unique Identifier.</p><p>EUI-64 turns a 48-bit MAC address into a 64-bit interface ID for an IPv6 address.</p></div>
  <div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option C</span> <strong>Extended Unique Identifier</strong>: EUI is the Extended Unique Identifier. The 64-bit version, EUI-64, is made from a MAC address and becomes the host part of an IPv6 address.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>Ethernet Unique Identifier</strong>: The word is Extended, not Ethernet.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>External Unique Identifier</strong>: The word is Extended, not External. The MAC address is extended from 48 to 64 bits.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>Extended Universal Identifier</strong>: The word is Unique, not Universal.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>EUI = Extended Unique Identifier. Think of it as the MAC address, extended to 64 bits.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1350';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: EUI-64 Conversion: Step 1</div><p>EUI-64 turns a 48-bit MAC address into a 64-bit interface ID. It is a method, not a type of address. The 3 steps are:</p><ul><li>1. Split the MAC address in half.</li><li>2. Insert FFFE in the middle.</li><li>3. Invert the 7th bit.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option A</span> <strong>Divide the MAC address in half</strong>: Step 1 is the split. A 48-bit MAC splits into two 24-bit halves, and FFFE goes between them in step 2.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>Invert the 7th bit</strong>: That is step 3, not step 1. The 7th bit is flipped only after FFFE is in place.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>Insert FFFE in the middle</strong>: That is step 2, not step 1. FFFE goes between the two halves after the MAC is split.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>Add FFFE to the end</strong>: FFFE goes in the middle of the split MAC, not at the end.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>EUI-64 in three steps: split, insert FFFE, flip the 7th bit. Step 1 is always the split.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1351';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: EUI-64 Conversion - Step 2</div><p>EUI-64 is a method of turning a 48-bit MAC address into a 64-bit interface ID. It has 3 steps:</p><ul><li>1. Split the MAC address in half</li><li>2. Insert FFFE in the middle</li><li>3. Invert the 7th bit</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option B</span> <strong>Insert FFFE in the middle</strong>: Step 2 is to insert FFFE in the middle. The two 24-bit halves of the MAC are joined with FFFE between them, making 64 bits.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>Insert FFFF in the middle</strong>: The value inserted is FFFE, not FFFF. The E in FFFE is what marks the inserted half.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>Divide the MAC address in half</strong>: That is step 1, not step 2. The MAC must already be split before FFFE can be inserted.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>Invert the 7th bit</strong>: That is step 3, not step 2. The bit is inverted only after FFFE is in place.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Step 2 = FFFE in the middle. F-F-F-E: the E is the giveaway that this is the EUI-64 insertion.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1352';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: EUI-64 Conversion - Step 3</div><p>EUI-64 is a method of turning a 48-bit MAC address into a 64-bit interface ID. The 3 steps are:</p><ul><li>1. Split the MAC address in half.</li><li>2. Insert FFFE in the middle.</li><li>3. Invert the 7th bit.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option B</span> <strong>Invert the 7th bit</strong>: Step 3 is to invert the 7th bit. Flip it: if it is 0, make it 1. If it is 1, make it 0.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>Insert FFFE in the middle</strong>: That is step 2, not step 3.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>Divide the MAC address in half</strong>: That is step 1, not step 3.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>Invert the 8th bit</strong>: It is the 7th bit that is inverted, not the 8th. The 8th bit is the I/G bit, which has a different job.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Step 3 = flip the 7th bit. The 7th bit is the U/L bit, and inverting it is the final step of EUI-64.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1353';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
  <div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 EUI-64 Address Configuration</div><p>To use EUI-64, give the router only the network prefix and add the keyword eui-64.</p><p>The router then builds the 64-bit interface ID from its own MAC address.</p></div>
  <div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option C</span> <strong>ipv6 address prefix/prefix-length eui-64</strong>: The command is ipv6 address prefix/prefix-length eui-64. You give only the prefix, and the router builds the host part itself.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>ipv6 enable</strong>: ipv6 enable only turns IPv6 on and gives a link-local address. It does not assign a global address with EUI-64.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>ipv6 address prefix/prefix-length</strong>: That is missing the eui-64 keyword. Without it, the router will not build the interface ID from the MAC address.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>ipv6 address address/prefix-length</strong>: That assigns a full static address you typed yourself. With eui-64 you give only the prefix, and the router builds the host part itself.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>EUI-64 on an interface = give the prefix, add the keyword eui-64, and let the router build the host part from its MAC.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1354';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: MAC Addresses: UAA</div><p>UAA stands for Universally Administered Address.</p><ul><li>It is the MAC address burned in by the manufacturer.</li><li>Its U/L bit is 0, which means universally administered.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option B</span> <strong>Universally Administered Address</strong>: That is the full expansion of UAA. It is the factory-assigned, globally unique MAC address.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>Universal Administered Address</strong>: The word is Universally, not Universal.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>Unique Administered Address</strong>: The word is Universally, not Unique.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>Universally Assigned Address</strong>: The word is Administered, not Assigned.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>UAA = Universally Administered Address. Burned in at the factory, U/L bit = 0.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1355';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: MAC Addresses - LAA</div><p>LAA stands for Locally Administered Address. It is a MAC address set locally by an administrator, with the U/L bit set to 1. The opposite is UAA, a Universally Administered Address assigned by the manufacturer.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option B</span> <strong>Locally Administered Address</strong>: LAA is the Locally Administered Address. It is a MAC address an administrator overrides locally, and its U/L bit is 1.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>Link Administered Address</strong>: The word is Locally, not Link. LAA has nothing to do with link-local addressing.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>Locally Assigned Address</strong>: The word is Administered, not Assigned. The address is administered locally, not merely assigned.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>Local Administered Address</strong>: The word is Locally, not Local. The full expansion is Locally Administered Address.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><ul><li>LAA = Locally Administered Address.</li><li>Set by the admin, U/L bit = 1.</li><li>The opposite of UAA.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1356';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: MAC Address - U/L Bit</div><p>The 7th bit of a MAC address is the U/L bit, short for Universal/Local. It tells you whether the address is universally administered (0) or locally administered (1).</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option D</span> <strong>U/L bit (Universal/Local bit)</strong>: The 7th bit is the U/L bit, short for Universal/Local. It is the bit EUI-64 inverts when it builds an interface ID.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>I/G bit (Individual/Group bit)</strong>: The I/G bit is the 8th bit, not the 7th. It marks unicast vs multicast.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>G/L bit (Global/Local bit)</strong>: There is no G/L bit. The correct name is the U/L bit, Universal/Local.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>U/M bit (Unicast/Multicast bit)</strong>: There is no U/M bit. Unicast vs multicast is the job of the I/G bit, the 8th bit.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><ul><li>7th bit = U/L bit.</li><li>8th bit = I/G bit.</li><li>The exam loves asking which bit is which.</li></ul></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1357';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
  <div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: MAC Addresses - U/L Bit Value for UAA</div><p>The U/L bit is the 7th bit of a MAC address.</p><p>In a MAC address, 0 means the address was assigned by the manufacturer.</p><p>That factory-assigned address is called a UAA, a Universally Administered Address.</p></div>
  <div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option B</span> <strong>0</strong>: The U/L bit is 0 for a UAA. A 0 in the U/L bit means the address was universally administered by the manufacturer.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>1</strong>: A U/L bit of 1 means the address is locally administered (LAA), not a UAA.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>FF</strong>: FF is a full byte value, not a single bit. The U/L bit can only be 0 or 1.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>0x01</strong>: 0x01 is a byte value of 1, which means the U/L bit is 1 (LAA), not 0 (UAA).</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>U/L bit 0 = UAA (factory), 1 = LAA (local). Zero is the factory default.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1358';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: MAC Addresses: U/L Bit</div><p>The U/L bit tells you which kind of MAC address it is.</p><ul><li>U/L bit 0 = UAA, Universally Administered Address.</li><li>U/L bit 1 = LAA, Locally Administered Address.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option D</span> <strong>U</strong>: A U/L bit of 0 means UAA. The letter is U, for Universally.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>G</strong>: There is no GAA. The two kinds are UAA and LAA.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>M</strong>: There is no MAA. The two kinds are UAA and LAA.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>L</strong>: L is for LAA, the Locally Administered Address. That has a U/L bit of 1, not 0.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>U/L bit 0 = UAA. The U is for Universally.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1359';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: MAC Addresses - U/L Bit Value for LAA</div><p>The U/L bit is the 7th bit of a MAC address. It tells you who set the address.</p><ul><li>U/L bit 1 = LAA, locally administered by an admin</li><li>U/L bit 0 = UAA, universally administered by the manufacturer</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option A</span> <strong>1</strong>: The U/L bit is 1 for an LAA. A 1 in the U/L bit means the address was locally administered, not factory-assigned.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>FF</strong>: FF is a full byte value, not a single bit. The U/L bit is one bit, so it can only be 0 or 1.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>0</strong>: A U/L bit of 0 means the address is universally administered (UAA), not an LAA.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>7</strong>: 7 is the position of the U/L bit (the 7th bit), not its value. The value itself is 1.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>U/L bit 1 = LAA (local). One is the admin''s mark.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1360';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: MAC Addresses - LAA Letter</div><p>The U/L bit tells you who gave the MAC address its value. If the bit is 1, the address is an LAA, a Locally Administered Address. The L in LAA is for Locally.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option B</span> <strong>L</strong>: A U/L bit of 1 means the address is an LAA. The letter is L, for Locally Administered Address.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>U</strong>: U is for UAA, Universally Administered Address. That has a U/L bit of 0.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>G</strong>: There is no GAA. The two kinds are UAA and LAA.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>M</strong>: There is no MAA. The two kinds are UAA and LAA.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>U/L bit 1 = LAA. The L is for Locally.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1361';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
  <div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Global Unicast Addresses</div><p>Global unicast addresses are the public IPv6 addresses.</p><p>They are globally unique and routable on the internet, just like public IPv4 addresses.</p></div>
  <div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option D</span> <strong>global unicast</strong>: Global unicast addresses are like public IPv4 addresses. They are unique worldwide and routable across the internet.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>unique local</strong>: Unique local addresses are like private IPv4 addresses. They are used only inside an organisation, not on the internet.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>link-local</strong>: Link-local addresses work only on one local link. They are never routed.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>multicast</strong>: A multicast address identifies a group of hosts, not one single host.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><ul><li>Global unicast = public.</li><li>Unique local = private.</li><li>Link-local = one link only.</li></ul></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1362';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Global Unicast Range</div><p>Global Unicast addresses are public IPv6 addresses. They work over the Internet, like public IPv4 addresses.</p><ul><li>The range was originally defined as 2000::/3.</li><li>Today it covers all addresses not reserved for other purposes.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option A</span> <strong>2000::/3 (now it includes all addresses which aren&#x27;t reserved for other purposes)</strong>: 2000::/3 was the original global unicast range. The first three bits, 001, mark an address as global unicast.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>FC00::/7</strong>: That is the unique local range, not global unicast.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>FF00::/8</strong>: That is the multicast range, not global unicast.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>FE80::/10</strong>: That is the link-local range, not global unicast.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Global unicast = 2000::/3. Know the other three ranges too: FE80::/10 link-local, FC00::/7 unique local, FF00::/8 multicast.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1363';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Global Unicast Structure</div><p>A Global Unicast address is a public IPv6 address. It has three parts, big to small, left to right:</p><ul><li>Global routing prefix, 48 bits, assigned by the ISP</li><li>Subnet identifier, 16 bits, used to make subnets</li><li>Interface identifier, 64 bits, the host part</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option C</span> <strong>Global routing prefix</strong>: The first section is the global routing prefix. It identifies the site or organisation on the internet, and the provider assigns it.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>Subnet identifier</strong>: The subnet identifier is the middle section. It identifies one subnet inside the site.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>Interface identifier</strong>: The interface identifier is the last section. It identifies one host interface on the subnet.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>Global ID</strong>: The global ID is a section of a unique local address, not of a global unicast address.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Global unicast = global routing prefix + subnet identifier + interface identifier. Big to small, left to right.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1364';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Global Unicast Structure</div><p>An IPv6 global unicast address, a GUA, has three parts:</p><ul><li>Global routing prefix, 48 bits, assigned by the ISP.</li><li>Subnet identifier, 16 bits, chosen by the site to number its own subnets.</li><li>Interface identifier, 64 bits, the host part.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option C</span> <strong>Subnet identifier</strong>: The middle section is the subnet identifier. The site uses it to divide its /48 block into many /64 subnets.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>Global ID</strong>: The global ID belongs to a unique local address, not to a global unicast address.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>Interface identifier</strong>: The interface identifier is the last section. It identifies one host interface.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>Global routing prefix</strong>: The global routing prefix is the first section. It is assigned by the provider.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Order: global routing prefix, subnet identifier, interface identifier. The middle one is the subnet identifier, and it is 16 bits long.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1365';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
  <div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Global Unicast Structure</div><p>A global unicast address has three parts.</p><ul><li>Global routing prefix: the network part, assigned by the ISP.</li><li>Subnet identifier: the subnet part, chosen by the site.</li><li>Interface identifier: the host part, usually 64 bits.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option A</span> <strong>Interface identifier</strong>: The last part is the interface identifier. It identifies one interface on the subnet, and it is usually 64 bits long.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>Subnet identifier</strong>: The subnet identifier is the middle part, chosen by the site.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>Global routing prefix</strong>: The global routing prefix is the first part, assigned by the provider.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>Global ID</strong>: The global ID belongs to a unique local address, not to a global unicast address.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Last part = interface identifier. It is the host part, 64 bits, often made by EUI-64.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1366';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Global Routing Prefix Size</div><p>A global unicast address has three parts.</p><ul><li>Global Routing Prefix: 48 bits, assigned by the ISP.</li><li>Subnet Identifier: 16 bits, used to make subnets.</li><li>Interface Identifier: 64 bits, the host part.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option B</span> <strong>48 bits</strong>: The global routing prefix is typically 48 bits. That is why an enterprise is given a /48 block.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>32 bits</strong>: 32 bits is the size of a block given to an ISP, not the enterprise routing prefix.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>64 bits</strong>: 64 bits is the size of the interface identifier, not the global routing prefix.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>16 bits</strong>: 16 bits is the size of the subnet identifier, not the global routing prefix.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>48 + 16 + 64 = 128. Routing prefix 48, subnet 16, interface 64.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1367';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Subnet Identifier Size</div><p>An enterprise usually gets a /48 block from its ISP, and IPv6 subnets usually use /64. That leaves 16 bits for subnets.</p><p>48-bit routing prefix + 16-bit subnet identifier + 64-bit interface identifier = 128 bits.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option A</span> <strong>16 bits</strong>: The subnet identifier is typically 16 bits. It is the middle part of the address, between the 48-bit routing prefix and the 64-bit interface identifier. With 16 bits a site can number 65,536 subnets.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>64 bits</strong>: 64 bits is the size of the interface identifier, not the subnet identifier.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>48 bits</strong>: 48 bits is the size of the global routing prefix, not the subnet identifier.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>40 bits</strong>: 40 bits is the size of the global ID in a unique local address, not the subnet identifier.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Subnet identifier = 16 bits. 48 routing + 16 subnet + 64 interface = 128.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1368';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Interface Identifier Size</div><p>The interface identifier of an IPv6 global unicast address is typically 64 bits. That matches the 64-bit EUI-64 value made from a MAC address. It is also the reason IPv6 subnets use a /64 prefix.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option C</span> <strong>64 bits</strong>: The interface identifier is typically 64 bits. It is the host part of the address, and the reason IPv6 subnets use a /64 prefix.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>128 bits</strong>: 128 bits is the length of the whole IPv6 address, not just the interface identifier.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>48 bits</strong>: 48 bits is the size of the global routing prefix, not the interface identifier.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>16 bits</strong>: 16 bits is the size of the subnet identifier, not the interface identifier.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Interface identifier = 64 bits. That is the whole reason subnets are /64.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1369';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
  <div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Unique Local Addresses</div><p>Unique local addresses are the private IPv6 addresses.</p><p>They are used only inside an organisation and are never routed on the public internet.</p><p>Your ISP will drop packets sent to a unique local address.</p></div>
  <div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option D</span> <strong>unique local</strong>: Unique local addresses are like private IPv4 addresses. They give a site addresses that are unique within that site but not reachable from the internet.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>global unicast</strong>: Global unicast addresses are like public IPv4 addresses. They are routable on the internet.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>multicast</strong>: A multicast address identifies a group of hosts, not one private host.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>link-local</strong>: Link-local addresses work only on one local link. They are not the private-address equivalent.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><ul><li>Unique local = private.</li><li>Global unicast = public.</li><li>Same idea as IPv4, new names.</li></ul></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1370';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Unique Local Range</div><p>Unique Local addresses are private IPv6 addresses. They work like private IPv4 addresses.</p><ul><li>They cannot be used over the Internet. Your ISP will drop packets sent to them.</li><li>The block reserved for them is FC00::/7.</li><li>The 8th bit must be 1, so in practice they all start with FD.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option D</span> <strong>FC00::/7</strong>: FC00::/7 is reserved for unique local addresses. The /7 covers FC00::/8 and FD00::/8, and FD is the part used in practice.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>2000::/3</strong>: That is the global unicast range, not unique local.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>FE80::/10</strong>: That is the link-local range, not unique local.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>FF00::/8</strong>: That is the multicast range, not unique local.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Unique local = FC00::/7. In practice the address starts with FD.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1371';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Unique Local Addresses</div><p>Unique Local addresses are private IPv6 addresses, like private IPv4 addresses. They cannot be used over the internet, and your ISP will drop packets sent to one.</p><p>The range is FC00::/7. But the 8th bit must be 1, so all Unique Local addresses actually start with FD.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option A</span> <strong>FD</strong>: Unique local addresses begin with FD. A real unique local address looks like FD00:..., FD12:..., and so on.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>FC</strong>: FC is the first half of the FC00::/7 block, but it is reserved and not used. Real unique local addresses start with FD.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>FE</strong>: FE is the start of link-local addresses (FE80::/10), not unique local addresses.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>FF</strong>: FF is the start of multicast addresses (FF00::/8), not unique local addresses.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Unique local = FD. If an address starts with FD, it is unique local.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1372';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Unique Local Structure</div><p>A unique local address has four parts:</p><ul><li>FD, the first part, which marks the address as unique local.</li><li>Global ID, 40 bits, randomly generated.</li><li>Subnet identifier, 16 bits.</li><li>Interface identifier, 64 bits.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option B</span> <strong>Hexadecimal FD (indicates a unique local address)</strong>: The first section is the hex value FD, which tells you the address is a unique local address.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>Subnet identifier</strong>: The subnet identifier is the third section, not the first.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>Global routing prefix</strong>: The global routing prefix is a section of a global unicast address, not of a unique local address.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>Interface identifier</strong>: The interface identifier is the last section, not the first.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Unique local starts with FD. That FD is the first of its four sections.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1373';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
  <div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Unique Local Structure</div><p>A unique local address has four parts.</p><ul><li>FD: the prefix that marks it as a unique local address.</li><li>Global ID: 40 bits, randomly generated.</li><li>Subnet identifier: 16 bits.</li><li>Interface identifier: 64 bits.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option D</span> <strong>Global ID</strong>: The second part is the global ID. It is 40 bits long and randomly generated, so each site gets a unique prefix.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>Subnet identifier</strong>: The subnet identifier is the third part, not the second.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>Interface identifier</strong>: The interface identifier is the last part, not the second.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>Global routing prefix</strong>: The global routing prefix belongs to a global unicast address. The unique local equivalent is the global ID.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Unique local part 2 = global ID, 40 bits, random. That randomness is what makes it unique.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1374';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Unique Local Structure</div><p>A unique local address has four parts.</p><ul><li>FD, the prefix.</li><li>Global ID, 40 bits, randomly generated.</li><li>Subnet Identifier, 16 bits.</li><li>Interface Identifier, 64 bits.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option C</span> <strong>Subnet identifier</strong>: The third section is the subnet identifier. It does the same job as in a global unicast address: the site uses it to divide its space into subnets.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>Global ID</strong>: The global ID is the second section, not the third.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>Interface identifier</strong>: The interface identifier is the last section, not the third.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>Global routing prefix</strong>: The global routing prefix belongs to a global unicast address, not to a unique local address.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Unique local, section 3 = subnet identifier. Same job as in global unicast.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1375';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Unique Local Structure</div><p>A Unique Local address has four parts:</p><ul><li>FD, the prefix</li><li>Global ID, 40 bits, randomly generated</li><li>Subnet identifier, 16 bits</li><li>Interface identifier, 64 bits, the host part</li></ul><p>The Global ID should be randomly generated, so subnets do not overlap if two companies merge.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option C</span> <strong>Interface identifier</strong>: The last section is the interface identifier. It is the host part of the address, 64 bits long, just as in a global unicast address.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>Subnet identifier</strong>: The subnet identifier is the third section, not the last.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>Global ID</strong>: The global ID is the second section, not the last.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>Global routing prefix</strong>: The global routing prefix belongs to a global unicast address, not to a unique local address.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Unique local section 4 = interface identifier, 64 bits. Last section, host part.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1376';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Unique Local Global ID Size</div><p>The global ID of a unique local address is 40 bits. Together with the 8-bit FD prefix, it makes the 48-bit site prefix. That leaves 16 bits for subnets and 64 bits for hosts.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option C</span> <strong>40 bits</strong>: The global ID is 40 bits long. It is randomly generated, which is what makes each site’s unique local prefix different from every other site’s.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>48 bits</strong>: 48 bits is the size of the whole site prefix, FD plus global ID, not the global ID alone.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>16 bits</strong>: 16 bits is the size of the subnet identifier, not the global ID.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>64 bits</strong>: 64 bits is the size of the interface identifier, not the global ID.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Global ID = 40 bits. 8 (FD) + 40 (global ID) = 48-bit site prefix, then 16 subnet + 64 interface.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1377';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
  <div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: Enabling IPv6 on an Interface</div><p>ipv6 enable turns IPv6 on for one interface, without you typing any address.</p><p>The interface then makes its own link-local address automatically.</p></div>
  <div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option B</span> <strong>ipv6 enable</strong>: The command is ipv6 enable, entered in interface configuration mode. It turns IPv6 on for that interface without assigning a specific address.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>ip routing</strong>: ip routing enables IPv4 routing globally. It has nothing to do with enabling IPv6 on an interface.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>ipv6 address eui-64</strong>: There is no command called ipv6 address eui-64. The EUI-64 form is ipv6 address prefix/prefix-length eui-64, and it does assign an address.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>ipv6 unicast-routing</strong>: ipv6 unicast-routing is a global command that enables IPv6 routing on the router. It does not enable IPv6 on one interface.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Just want IPv6 on, no address typed? ipv6 enable on the interface. Want the router to forward IPv6? ipv6 unicast-routing globally.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1378';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Link-Local Range</div><p>Link-local addresses are made automatically on an IPv6-enabled interface. You do not configure them yourself.</p><ul><li>The block is FE80::/10, and a link-local address always starts with FE80.</li><li>Link-local means one link only. Routers will not forward packets sent to a link-local destination.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option C</span> <strong>FE80::/10</strong>: FE80::/10 is reserved for link-local addresses. Every IPv6-enabled interface gets one automatically.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>2000::/3</strong>: That is the global unicast range, not link-local.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>FC00::/7</strong>: That is the unique local range, not link-local.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>FF00::/8</strong>: That is the multicast range, not link-local.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Link-local = FE80::/10. If an address starts with FE80, it stays on its own link.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1379';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Link-Local Interface ID</div><p>Link-local addresses are automatically generated on an IPv6-enabled interface. You do not configure them yourself.</p><p>The range is FE80::/10, so a link-local address always starts with FE80. The interface ID is made with EUI-64 from the MAC address. The link-local address and the Global Unicast address on the same interface use the same interface ID.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option C</span> <strong>EUI-64</strong>: The interface ID is generated by EUI-64. The link-local address is FE80::/10 plus a 64-bit interface ID made from the MAC address.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>Manual configuration</strong>: A link-local address is generated automatically. Manual configuration is for global or unique local addresses.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>DHCPv6</strong>: DHCPv6 hands out addresses from a server. A link-local address is generated locally on the device, not from a server.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>NDP</strong>: NDP is the protocol that uses link-local addresses, but it does not generate the interface ID. EUI-64 does.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Link-local interface ID = EUI-64. Same mechanism as global addresses, just with an FE80 prefix.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1380';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Link-Local Addresses</div><p>Link-local addresses always start with FE80. The full link-local block is FE80::/10, but the 54 bits after FE80::/10 must all be 0, so the first quartet you actually see is always FE80.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option C</span> <strong>FE80</strong>: Link-local addresses begin with FE80. A typical link-local address looks like FE80::1.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>2000</strong>: 2000 is the start of the global unicast range (2000::/3), not link-local.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>FC00</strong>: FC00 is the start of the unique local range (FC00::/7), not link-local.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>FF02</strong>: FF02 is the start of link-local multicast addresses. FF02::1 is all nodes, not a link-local unicast address.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>FE80 = link-local. If an address starts with FE80, it never leaves its own link.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1381';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
  <div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Unicast Addresses</div><p>Unicast means one-to-one.</p><p>One unicast address identifies one single interface, so a packet sent to it goes to exactly that host.</p></div>
  <div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option B</span> <strong>Uni</strong>: Uni means one. A unicast address is assigned to one interface, so communication is one-to-one.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>Any</strong>: Anycast is one-to-one-of-many communication, sent to the nearest member of a group.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>Broad</strong>: Broadcast is one-to-all communication, and IPv6 does not use broadcast at all.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>Multi</strong>: Multicast is one-to-many communication, sent to a group of hosts.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Uni = one. Broadcast = all, multicast = many, anycast = nearest one of many.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1382';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: Cast Types: One-to-All</div><p>There are four cast types. Learn them by how many destinations they reach.</p><ul><li>Unicast: one-to-one, one source to one destination.</li><li>Broadcast: one-to-all, one source to every host.</li><li>Multicast: one-to-many, one source to a group that joined.</li><li>Anycast: one-to-one-of-many, one source to the nearest of many.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option A</span> <strong>Broad</strong>: Broadcast means one-to-all. One packet goes to every host on the link.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>Uni</strong>: Unicast is one-to-one, sent to a single host.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>Any</strong>: Anycast is one-to-one-of-many, sent to the nearest member of a group.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>Multi</strong>: Multicast is one-to-many, sent to a group of hosts that joined.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Broad = all. Note that IPv6 has no broadcast. FF02::1, the all-nodes multicast address, does the job instead.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1383';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Multicast Addresses</div><ul><li>Unicast = one-to-one. One source, one destination.</li><li>Broadcast = one-to-all.</li><li>Multicast = one-to-many. One source, many destinations that joined the group.</li><li>Anycast = one-to-one-of-many. One source, the nearest one out of many.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option C</span> <strong>Multi</strong>: Multi means many. A multicast packet goes to all members of a group at once, which is one-to-many communication.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>Any</strong>: Anycast is one-to-one-of-many communication, sent to the nearest member of a group.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>Broad</strong>: Broadcast is one-to-all communication, sent to every host on the link. IPv6 does not use broadcast at all.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>Uni</strong>: Unicast is one-to-one communication, sent to a single host.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Multi = many. One packet, many receivers, all in the same multicast group.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1384';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Anycast Addresses</div><p>Anycast is one-to-one-of-many. The same anycast address is given to several hosts, and the network delivers the packet to the nearest one.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option D</span> <strong>Any</strong>: Any means any one of the group. An anycast packet goes to the nearest host that shares the address, which is one-to-one-of-many.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>Multi</strong>: Multicast is one-to-many. The packet goes to every member of the group.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>Broad</strong>: Broadcast is one-to-all. The packet goes to every host on the link.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>Uni</strong>: Unicast is one-to-one. The packet goes to one specific host.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Anycast = the nearest one of many. Same address on several servers, and the network picks the closest.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1385';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
  <div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Multicast Range</div><p>Multicast addresses always start with FF.</p><p>The range reserved for multicast is FF00::/8.</p></div>
  <div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option D</span> <strong>FF00::/8</strong>: FF00::/8 is reserved for multicast. Any IPv6 address beginning with FF is a multicast address.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>FC00::/7</strong>: FC00::/7 is the unique local range, not multicast.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>2000::/3</strong>: 2000::/3 is the global unicast range, not multicast.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>FE80::/10</strong>: FE80::/10 is the link-local range, not multicast.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Multicast = FF00::/8. FF at the start always means multicast.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1386';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Broadcast</div><p>IPv6 does not use broadcast. There is no broadcast address in IPv6.</p><ul><li>Anything that used broadcast in IPv4 now uses multicast.</li><li>Example: ARP used broadcast in IPv4. In IPv6, NDP uses multicast instead.</li><li>The all-nodes multicast address, FF02::1, works like a broadcast on the link.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option D</span> <strong>No</strong>: No, IPv6 does not use broadcast anywhere. Multicast does the job instead.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>Only for multicast</strong>: Multicast is a different thing from broadcast. IPv6 uses multicast, not broadcast, in every case.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>Yes</strong>: IPv6 has no broadcast address and no broadcast traffic. That is one of the big differences from IPv4.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>Only for link-local</strong>: IPv6 does not use broadcast on a single link either. Multicast handles link-local group traffic.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>IPv6 has no broadcast. None. Ever. Multicast does the job instead.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1387';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Multicast - All Nodes</div><p>IPv6 does NOT use broadcast. The all-nodes multicast address works like a broadcast: it sends to all hosts in the subnet. FF02 is the link-local scope.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option C</span> <strong>FF02::1</strong>: FF02::1 is the all-nodes multicast address. It is used to reach every node on the local link at once.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>FF00::1</strong>: FF00::1 is not the all-nodes address. The all-nodes address is in the FF02 (link-local) scope, not FF00.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>FF02::2</strong>: FF02::2 is the all-routers multicast address, not all nodes. It reaches only the routers on the link.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>FE80::1</strong>: FE80::1 is a link-local unicast address, not a multicast address. It reaches one host only.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>FF02::1 = all nodes, FF02::2 = all routers. The last digit is the difference: 1 for nodes, 2 for routers.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1388';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Well-Known Multicast Addresses</div><p>IPv6 has no broadcast. Instead, hosts and routers use well-known link-local multicast addresses in the FF02:: range. A packet sent to one of these addresses reaches a whole group at once. Each address is reserved for one specific group.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option C</span> <strong>All nodes</strong>: FF02::1 is the well-known multicast address reserved for All nodes.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>All EIGRP routers</strong>: That is the multicast group for FF02::A, not All nodes.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>All routers</strong>: That is the multicast group for FF02::2, not All nodes.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>All OSPF routers</strong>: That is the multicast group for FF02::5, not All nodes.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Memorize the FF02:: series by the last digit: ::1 all nodes, ::2 all routers, ::5 OSPF (Open Shortest Path First) routers, ::6 OSPF DR (Designated Router)/BDR (Backup Designated Router), ::9 RIP (Routing Information Protocol), ::A EIGRP (Enhanced Interior Gateway Routing Protocol). The exam loves asking them both ways round.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1389';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Well-Known Multicast Addresses</div><p>IPv6 has no broadcast.</p><p>Instead, devices use well-known link-local multicast addresses in the FF02 range.</p><p>Each address reaches one specific group.</p></div>
  <div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option D</span> <strong>FF02::2</strong>: <strong>FF02::2</strong> is the well-known multicast address reserved for <strong>All routers</strong>.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>FF02::1</strong>: This address belongs to the <strong>All nodes</strong> group, not All routers.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>FF02::5</strong>: This address belongs to the <strong>All OSPF routers</strong> group, not All routers.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>FF02::9</strong>: This address belongs to the <strong>All RIP routers</strong> group, not All routers.</li></ul></div>
<div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Memorise the FF02 series by the last digit: ::1 all nodes, ::2 all routers, ::5 OSPF routers, ::6 OSPF DR/BDR, ::9 RIP, ::A EIGRP. The exam loves asking them both ways round.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1390';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Well-Known Multicast Addresses</div><p>IPv6 has no broadcast, so routers and hosts use well-known link-local multicast addresses. Each one is reserved for one group.</p><ul><li>FF02::1 = all nodes, all hosts.</li><li>FF02::2 = all routers.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option B</span> <strong>All routers</strong>: FF02::2 is the well-known multicast address for all routers.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>All nodes</strong>: That is FF02::1, not FF02::2.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>All RIP routers</strong>: That is FF02::9, not FF02::2.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>All OSPF routers</strong>: That is FF02::5, not FF02::2.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Memorise the FF02:: series by the last digit: ::1 all nodes, ::2 all routers, ::5 OSPF routers, ::6 OSPF DR/BDR, ::9 RIP, ::A EIGRP. The IPv4 and IPv6 versions use the same last digit.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1391';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Well-Known Multicast Addresses</div><p>IPv6 has no broadcast. Routers and hosts use well-known link-local multicast addresses in the FF02:: range, so one packet reaches a whole group at once. Each address is reserved for one specific group.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option C</span> <strong>FF02::5</strong>: FF02::5 is the well-known multicast address reserved for All OSPF routers.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>FF02::A</strong>: This address belongs to the All EIGRP routers group, not All OSPF routers.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>FF02::6</strong>: This address belongs to the All OSPF DRs/BDRs group, not All OSPF routers.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>FF02::2</strong>: This address belongs to the All routers group, not All OSPF routers.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Memorise the FF02:: series by the last digit: ::1 all nodes, ::2 all routers, ::5 OSPF routers, ::6 OSPF DR/BDR, ::9 RIP, ::A EIGRP. The exam loves asking them both ways round.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1392';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Well-Known Multicast Addresses</div><p>IPv6 has no broadcast. Instead, hosts and routers use well-known link-local multicast addresses in the FF02:: range. A packet sent to one of these addresses reaches a whole group at once. Each address is reserved for one specific group.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option B</span> <strong>All OSPF routers</strong>: FF02::5 is the well-known multicast address reserved for All OSPF routers.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>All routers</strong>: That is the multicast group for FF02::2, not All OSPF routers.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>All OSPF DRs/BDRs</strong>: That is the multicast group for FF02::6, not All OSPF routers.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>All EIGRP routers</strong>: That is the multicast group for FF02::A, not All OSPF routers.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Memorize the FF02:: series by the last digit: ::1 all nodes, ::2 all routers, ::5 OSPF (Open Shortest Path First) routers, ::6 OSPF DR (Designated Router)/BDR (Backup Designated Router), ::9 RIP (Routing Information Protocol), ::A EIGRP (Enhanced Interior Gateway Routing Protocol). The exam loves asking them both ways round.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1393';
