UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Well-Known Multicast Addresses</div><p>IPv6 has no broadcast, so routers and hosts use well-known link-local multicast addresses. Each one is reserved for one group.</p><ul><li>FF02::5 = all OSPF routers.</li><li>FF02::6 = all OSPF DRs/BDRs.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option D</span> <strong>All OSPF DRs/BDRs</strong>: FF02::6 is the well-known multicast address for all OSPF DRs/BDRs.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>All OSPF routers</strong>: That is FF02::5, not FF02::6.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>All nodes</strong>: That is FF02::1, not FF02::6.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>All RIP routers</strong>: That is FF02::9, not FF02::6.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Memorise the FF02:: series by the last digit: ::1 all nodes, ::2 all routers, ::5 OSPF routers, ::6 OSPF DR/BDR, ::9 RIP, ::A EIGRP. The IPv4 and IPv6 versions use the same last digit.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1395';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Well-Known Multicast Addresses</div><p>IPv6 has no broadcast. Routers and hosts use well-known link-local multicast addresses in the FF02:: range, so one packet reaches a whole group at once. Each address is reserved for one specific group.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option B</span> <strong>FF02::9</strong>: FF02::9 is the well-known multicast address reserved for All RIP routers.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>FF02::2</strong>: This address belongs to the All routers group, not All RIP routers.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>FF02::5</strong>: This address belongs to the All OSPF routers group, not All RIP routers.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>FF02::A</strong>: This address belongs to the All EIGRP routers group, not All RIP routers.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Memorise the FF02:: series by the last digit: ::1 all nodes, ::2 all routers, ::5 OSPF routers, ::6 OSPF DR/BDR, ::9 RIP, ::A EIGRP. The exam loves asking them both ways round.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1396';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Well-Known Multicast Addresses</div><p>IPv6 has no broadcast. Instead, hosts and routers use well-known link-local multicast addresses in the FF02:: range. A packet sent to one of these addresses reaches a whole group at once. Each address is reserved for one specific group.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option C</span> <strong>All RIP routers</strong>: FF02::9 is the well-known multicast address reserved for All RIP routers.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>All EIGRP routers</strong>: That is the multicast group for FF02::A, not All RIP routers.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>All OSPF routers</strong>: That is the multicast group for FF02::5, not All RIP routers.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>All routers</strong>: That is the multicast group for FF02::2, not All RIP routers.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Memorize the FF02:: series by the last digit: ::1 all nodes, ::2 all routers, ::5 OSPF (Open Shortest Path First) routers, ::6 OSPF DR (Designated Router)/BDR (Backup Designated Router), ::9 RIP (Routing Information Protocol), ::A EIGRP (Enhanced Interior Gateway Routing Protocol). The exam loves asking them both ways round.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1397';
