UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Well-Known Multicast Addresses</div><p>IPv6 has no broadcast, so routers and hosts use well-known link-local multicast addresses. Each one is reserved for one group.</p><ul><li>FF02::A = all EIGRP routers.</li><li>Hex A = decimal 10, the same last digit as IPv4 224.0.0.10.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option B</span> <strong>All EIGRP routers</strong>: FF02::A is the well-known multicast address for all EIGRP routers.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>All nodes</strong>: That is FF02::1, not FF02::A.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>All RIP routers</strong>: That is FF02::9, not FF02::A.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>All OSPF DRs/BDRs</strong>: That is FF02::6, not FF02::A.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Memorise the FF02:: series by the last digit: ::1 all nodes, ::2 all routers, ::5 OSPF routers, ::6 OSPF DR/BDR, ::9 RIP, ::A EIGRP. The IPv4 and IPv6 versions use the same last digit.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1399';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv4 Well-Known Multicast Addresses</div><p>IPv4 multicast uses 224.0.0.0/24 for link-local multicast. Routing protocols each listen on their own well-known address, so a router can send one packet to every router running the same protocol on the link.</p><p>The IPv4 and IPv6 versions use the same last digit. EIGRP is FF02::A in IPv6, and hex A is decimal 10.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option D</span> <strong>224.0.0.10</strong>: 224.0.0.10 is the well-known multicast address reserved for All EIGRP routers.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>224.0.0.5</strong>: This address belongs to the All OSPF routers group, not All EIGRP routers.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>224.0.0.9</strong>: This address belongs to the All RIP routers group, not All EIGRP routers.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>224.0.0.6</strong>: This address belongs to the All OSPF DRs/BDRs group, not All EIGRP routers.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Learn the 224.0.0.x set together: .1 all nodes, .2 all routers, .5 OSPF, .6 OSPF DR/BDR, .9 RIP, .10 EIGRP.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1400';
