UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Multicast Scope</div><p>The 4th hex character of a multicast address tells you its scope. The scope limits how far the packet travels.</p><ul><li>FF02 = link-local. FF05 = site-local.</li><li>FF08 = organization-local. It is wider than site-local, for example all subnets in the company.</li><li>FF0E = global. It can be routed over the public Internet.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option D</span> <strong>Organization-local</strong>: FF08 means organization-local scope.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>Global</strong>: That is the FF0E scope, not FF08.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>Site-local</strong>: That is the FF05 scope, not FF08.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>Link-local</strong>: That is the FF02 scope, not FF08.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Read the scope from the 4th hex character: 1 interface, 2 link, 5 site, 8 organization, E global.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1419';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Multicast Scope</div><p>The second byte of an IPv6 multicast address (FF0x) carries the scope. The scope limits how far the packet travels.</p><ul><li>1 = interface-local, the message does not leave the local device</li><li>2 = link-local, stays in the local subnet</li><li>5 = site-local, only within one physical site</li><li>8 = organization-local, wider than site-local</li><li>E = global, can be routed over the public internet</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option A</span> <strong>FF0E</strong>: FF0E is the scope value for Global.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>FF05</strong>: This is the scope value for Site-local, not Global.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>FF08</strong>: This is the scope value for Organization-local, not Global.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>FF02</strong>: This is the scope value for Link-local, not Global.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Read the scope from the second byte: FF01 interface, FF02 link, FF05 site, FF08 organization, FF0E global.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1420';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Multicast Scope</div><p>The second byte of an IPv6 multicast address (FF0x) carries the scope. Scope says how far the packet can travel. 1 is the interface itself, 2 is the local link, 5 is the site, 8 is the organization, and E (14) is global.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option C</span> <strong>Global</strong>: FF0E means Global scope. It can be routed over the public Internet. There are no boundaries.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>Link-local</strong>: That is the FF02 scope, not Global.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>Organization-local</strong>: That is the FF08 scope, not Global.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>Site-local</strong>: That is the FF05 scope, not Global.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Read the scope from the second byte: FF01 interface, FF02 link, FF05 site, FF08 organization, FF0E global.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1421';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
  <div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Anycast Addressing</div><p>An anycast address is given to several devices at once.</p><p>A packet sent to it goes to the nearest one.</p><p>There is no special range for anycast. You use a normal unicast address and mark it as anycast.</p></div>
  <div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option C</span> <strong>ipv6 address address/prefix-length anycast</strong>: The anycast keyword tells the router this address is an anycast address, not a normal unicast address.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>ipv6 address address/prefix-length</strong>: This configures a normal unicast address, not an anycast address.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>ipv6 address autoconfig</strong>: Autoconfig enables SLAAC, which creates unicast addresses, not anycast addresses.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>ipv6 address address/prefix-length eui-64</strong>: The eui-64 keyword builds the interface ID from the MAC address. It does not make the address anycast.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Anycast = same address on many devices, nearest one answers. The keyword anycast is the giveaway in the command.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1422';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Special Addresses</div><p>The unspecified address is ::, all zeros.</p><ul><li>A device uses it when it does not know its IPv6 address yet.</li><li>For example, a host uses :: as its source address while doing Duplicate Address Detection.</li><li>The IPv6 default route is ::/0, like 0.0.0.0/0 in IPv4.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option C</span> <strong>::</strong>: :: is the all-zeros address, the IPv6 unspecified address.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>::1</strong>: That is the loopback address, not the unspecified address.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>FE80::/10</strong>: That is the link-local prefix, not the unspecified address.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>FF02::1</strong>: That is the all-nodes multicast address, not the unspecified address.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>:: means "I have no address yet". ::1 means "myself", the loopback. Do not mix them up.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1423';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Special Addresses</div><p>The IPv6 loopback address is ::1, the equivalent of 127.0.0.1 in IPv4. It lets a device test its own protocol stack. Messages to ::1 are processed inside the local device, not sent to other devices, and it is never assigned to a real interface.</p><p>The unspecified address is :: (all zeros). It is used when a device does not know its IPv6 address yet.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option C</span> <strong>::1</strong>: ::1 is the IPv6 loopback address.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>::</strong>: This is the unspecified address, not the loopback address.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>FE80::1</strong>: This is a link-local address, not the loopback address.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>127.0.0.1</strong>: This is the IPv4 loopback address, not the IPv6 one.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>::1 = loopback, :: = unspecified. The single 1 at the end is the difference.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 32 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 2)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1424';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Header Fields</div><p>The IPv6 header starts with the Version field, 4 bits. For IPv6 it is always 6, written in binary as 0110. That is how a receiver knows the packet is IPv6 and not IPv4.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option A</span> <strong>Version</strong>: The Version field carries the IP version number, which is 6 for IPv6.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>Flow Label</strong>: This field identifies a traffic flow for QoS handling, not the IP version.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>Traffic Class</strong>: This field carries QoS marking, not the IP version.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>Payload Length</strong>: This field gives the length of the data after the header, not the IP version.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>The first field of any IP header is always Version. IPv4 = 4, IPv6 = 6.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1425';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
  <div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Header Fields</div><p>The IPv6 header is fixed at 40 bytes.</p><p>Traffic Class is its QoS field, 8 bits.</p><p>It gives priority to high-priority traffic like voice and video.</p></div>
  <div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option D</span> <strong>Traffic Class</strong>: Traffic Class is the QoS field of the IPv6 header.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>Next Header</strong>: This identifies the next protocol, like TCP or UDP, not QoS.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>Flow Label</strong>: This identifies one traffic flow, not the QoS marking.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>Hop Limit</strong>: This limits how many routers a packet can cross, like IPv4 TTL, not QoS.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><ul><li>Traffic Class = QoS.</li><li>Flow Label = which flow.</li><li>Next Header = what protocol follows.</li></ul></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1426';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Header Fields</div><p>The Flow Label is a 20-bit field in the IPv6 header.</p><ul><li>A source marks all packets of one flow with the same label.</li><li>Routers can then treat that flow the same way, without looking at the transport-layer ports.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option A</span> <strong>Flow Label</strong>: The Flow Label identifies one traffic flow between a source and a destination.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>Traffic Class</strong>: Traffic Class carries the QoS marking. It gives priority to traffic like voice and video, but it does not identify a flow.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>Payload Length</strong>: Payload Length is the length of the data after the header. It does not identify a flow.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>Next Header</strong>: Next Header tells you the next protocol, like TCP or UDP. It does not identify a flow.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Flow Label = "these packets belong together". It is the only header field with no IPv4 equivalent.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1427';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Header Fields</div><p>The IPv6 header is fixed at 40 bytes. There is no header-length field, and routers process it faster than the IPv4 header.</p><p>Payload Length is a 16-bit field. It gives the length, in bytes, of everything after the 40-byte header, including the transport header and data. The header itself is not counted.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option A</span> <strong>Payload Length</strong>: Payload Length is the length of the encapsulated Layer 4 segment and data after the header.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>Hop Limit</strong>: This limits router hops, like IPv4 TTL. Each router subtracts 1, and the packet is dropped at 0. It is not a length.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>Flow Label</strong>: This identifies one traffic flow, so routers treat its packets the same. It is not a length.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>Traffic Class</strong>: This carries QoS marking and gives priority to high-priority traffic like voice and video. It is not a length.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Payload Length = how much data follows the header. Header length is fixed at 40 bytes, so it is never in this field.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1428';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Header Fields</div><p>The IPv6 header is fixed at 40 bytes. The IPv4 header is variable, 20 to 60 bytes. Because the IPv6 header is always 40 bytes, there is no header length field, and routers process it faster.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option A</span> <strong>40 bytes</strong>: The IPv6 header is always 40 bytes.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>60 bytes</strong>: That is the maximum IPv4 header size with options, not the IPv6 header.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>20 bytes</strong>: That is the size of an IPv4 header with no options, not an IPv6 header.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>32 bytes</strong>: That is only the two 16-byte IPv6 addresses added together, not the whole header.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><ul><li>IPv6 header = 40 bytes, fixed.</li><li>IPv4 header = 20 bytes minimum.</li><li>The exam tests this pair often.</li></ul></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1429';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
  <div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Header Fields</div><p>Next Header is an 8-bit field in the IPv6 header.</p><p>It tells the receiver which protocol comes next, for example 6 for TCP, 17 for UDP, or 58 for ICMPv6.</p><p>It does the same job as the Protocol field in IPv4.</p></div>
  <div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option C</span> <strong>Next Header</strong>: Next Header tells the receiver which Layer 4 protocol is inside the packet.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>Traffic Class</strong>: This carries the QoS marking, not the protocol.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>Payload Length</strong>: This gives the length of the data after the header, not the protocol.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>Hop Limit</strong>: This limits router hops, like IPv4 TTL. It does not identify the next protocol.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Next Header = the IPv6 version of the IPv4 Protocol field. 6 = TCP, 17 = UDP, 58 = ICMPv6.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1430';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Header Fields</div><p>Hop Limit is an 8-bit field. Every router the packet crosses takes 1 away from it.</p><ul><li>When it reaches 0, the packet is dropped. That stops routing loops.</li><li>It does the same job as the IPv4 TTL field, just under a new name.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option C</span> <strong>Hop Limit</strong>: Hop Limit works exactly like IPv4 TTL. It limits how many routers a packet can cross.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>Payload Length</strong>: Payload Length is the length of the data after the header, not the hop count.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>Flow Label</strong>: Flow Label identifies a traffic flow, not the hop count.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>Next Header</strong>: Next Header tells you the next protocol, not the hop count.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Hop Limit = TTL with a new name. Every router subtracts 1. At 0 the packet is dropped.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1431';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Solicited-Node Multicast Address</div><p>Every IPv6 unicast address has a matching solicited-node multicast address. It is built from a fixed prefix plus the last 6 hex digits (last 24 bits) of the unicast address.</p><p>Example: unicast ...:36:8500 gives solicited-node FF02::1:FF36:8500. Every IPv6 interface joins its own solicited-node multicast group.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option C</span> <strong>ff02::1:ff</strong>: The solicited-node address is FF02::1:FF plus the last 6 hex digits of the unicast address.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>ff02::2:ff</strong>: The prefix is FF02::1:FF, not FF02::2:FF.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>fe80::1:ff</strong>: The prefix is FF02::1:FF, a multicast prefix, not a link-local FE80 prefix.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>ff02::1:fe</strong>: The prefix is FF02::1:FF, not FE.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Solicited-node = FF02::1:FF + last 6 hex digits of the address. Remember the FF at the end of the prefix.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1432';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Solicited-Node Multicast Address</div><p>A solicited-node multicast address is made from a unicast address. Take the fixed prefix FF02::1:FF and add the last 6 hex digits of the unicast address. Example: a unicast address ending in ...36:8500 gives FF02::1:FF36:8500.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option A</span> <strong>Last 6 hex digits of unicast address</strong>: Only the last 6 hex digits, 24 bits, of the unicast address are copied into the solicited-node address.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>First 6 hex digits of the unicast address</strong>: It is the LAST 6 hex digits, not the first 6.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>The full 128-bit unicast address</strong>: Only the last 24 bits are used, not the whole address.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>Last 6 hex digits of the MAC address</strong>: The address is built from the unicast IPv6 address, not the MAC address.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Last 6 hex digits only. That is why the solicited-node group is small and efficient.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1433';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
  <div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: Neighbor Discovery Protocol (NDP)</div><p>NDP replaces ARP in IPv6. IPv6 does not use ARP.</p><p>NDP runs over ICMPv6 and uses solicited-node multicast addresses.</p><p>Its three jobs are: learn MAC addresses, discover routers, and check for duplicate addresses.</p></div>
  <div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option D</span> <strong>Neighbor Discovery Protocol</strong>: NDP stands for Neighbor Discovery Protocol.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>Neighbor Detection Protocol</strong>: The word is Discovery, not Detection.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>Network Discovery Protocol</strong>: The word is Neighbor, not Network.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>Network Distribution Protocol</strong>: Neither word is right. It is Neighbor Discovery, not Network Distribution.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>NDP = Neighbor Discovery Protocol. It replaces ARP in IPv6.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1434';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: Neighbor Discovery Protocol (NDP)</div><p>IPv6 does not use ARP. NDP replaces ARP.</p><ul><li>NDP uses ICMPv6 messages to learn MAC addresses.</li><li>It is more efficient than ARP. An ARP request is a broadcast to all hosts, but an NDP message is a multicast to one specific host.</li><li>NDP also discovers routers and checks for duplicate addresses.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option A</span> <strong>NDP</strong>: NDP replaces ARP in IPv6. It learns a neighbour MAC address with a Neighbor Solicitation and a Neighbor Advertisement.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>ICMPv6</strong>: NDP runs over ICMPv6, but the protocol that replaces ARP is NDP itself.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>SLAAC</strong>: SLAAC makes addresses automatically. It does not resolve MAC addresses.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>DHCPv6</strong>: DHCPv6 hands out addresses and options. It does not resolve MAC addresses.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>No ARP in IPv6. NDP does address resolution with NS and NA messages.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1435';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: NDP Message Types</div><p>NDP replaces ARP in IPv6. IPv6 does NOT use ARP. NDP has four ICMPv6 messages:</p><ul><li>RS, type 133, asks: are there any routers on this link?</li><li>RA, type 134, a router answers: I am here, here is the prefix</li><li>NS, type 135, asks: who has this address?</li><li>NA, type 136, answers with the MAC address</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option B</span> <strong>Neighbor Solicitation</strong>: NS stands for Neighbor Solicitation. It is the NDP version of an ARP request.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>Router Advertisement</strong>: That is RA, sent by a router, not a host looking for a neighbour.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>Router Solicitation</strong>: That is RS, used to find a router, not a neighbour.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>Neighbor Advertisement</strong>: That is NA, the answer to a solicitation, not the question.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>NS = Neighbor Solicitation, type 135. NA = the reply, type 136.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1436';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: NDP Message Types</div><p>NDP replaces ARP in IPv6. A Neighbor Advertisement (NA) is the reply to a Neighbor Solicitation (NS). It tells the requester the MAC address of the node that owns the asked-for IPv6 address.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option A</span> <strong>Neighbor Advertisement</strong>: NA stands for Neighbor Advertisement. It is the reply that carries the MAC address back to the requester.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>Neighbor Solicitation</strong>: That is NS, the request, not the reply.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>Router Advertisement</strong>: That is RA, sent by a router, not a host answering an NS.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>Router Solicitation</strong>: That is RS, used to find a router, not to answer an NS.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>NA = Neighbor Advertisement, type 136. It answers an NS (type 135).</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1437';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
  <div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: NDP Message Types</div><p>A host sends a Router Solicitation when it wants to find a router on the link.</p><p>For example, it needs the network prefix so it can build its own address with SLAAC.</p><p>The RS goes to FF02::2, the all-routers address.</p></div>
  <div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option A</span> <strong>Router Solicitation</strong>: RS stands for Router Solicitation.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>Neighbor Solicitation</strong>: That is NS. It asks for a neighbour''s MAC address, not for a router.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>Router Advertisement</strong>: That is RA, the router''s reply, not the host''s request.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>Neighbor Advertisement</strong>: That is NA, the reply to an NS, not a request for a router.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>RS = Router Solicitation, type 133. The host asks, and the router answers with an RA.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1438';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: NDP Message Types</div><p>RA stands for Router Advertisement. It is sent by a router.</p><ul><li>It is sent in reply to an RS, and also sent on its own, from time to time.</li><li>It tells hosts the prefix of the network, so they can build their own address with SLAAC.</li><li>Hosts learn their default gateway from RA messages.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option D</span> <strong>Router Advertisement</strong>: RA stands for Router Advertisement, ICMPv6 Type 134.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>Neighbor Solicitation</strong>: That is NS, Type 135. It asks for a neighbour MAC address, not a router message.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>Neighbor Advertisement</strong>: That is NA, Type 136. It is the reply to an NS, not a router message.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>Router Solicitation</strong>: That is RS, Type 133. It is the host asking for a router, not the router reply.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>RA = Router Advertisement, Type 134. Do not mix up NS/NA, for MAC addresses, with RS/RA, for finding routers.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1439';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: NDP Message Types</div><p>Each NDP message is an ICMPv6 packet with a fixed type number. Do not mix up NS/NA (for MAC addresses) with RS/RA (for finding routers).</p><ul><li>133 = RS, Router Solicitation</li><li>134 = RA, Router Advertisement</li><li>135 = NS, Neighbor Solicitation</li><li>136 = NA, Neighbor Advertisement</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option D</span> <strong>NS</strong>: NS (Neighbor Solicitation) is ICMPv6 Type 135.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>NA</strong>: NA (Neighbor Advertisement) is Type 136, the reply to an NS.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>RS</strong>: RS (Router Solicitation) is Type 133, used to find a router.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>RA</strong>: RA (Router Advertisement) is Type 134, sent by a router.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>NDP types in order: 133 RS, 134 RA, 135 NS, 136 NA. Remember 133-134 routers, 135-136 neighbours.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1440';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: NDP Message Types</div><p>NDP messages are ICMPv6 packets, and each one has a fixed type number:</p><ul><li>133 RS, Router Solicitation.</li><li>134 RA, Router Advertisement.</li><li>135 NS, Neighbor Solicitation.</li><li>136 NA, Neighbor Advertisement.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option B</span> <strong>135</strong>: NS, Neighbor Solicitation, is ICMPv6 type 135. It is the NDP version of an ARP request: Hi, what is your MAC address?</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>133</strong>: 133 is Router Solicitation, RS, not Neighbor Solicitation.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>134</strong>: 134 is Router Advertisement, RA, not Neighbor Solicitation.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>136</strong>: 136 is Neighbor Advertisement, NA, not Neighbor Solicitation.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>NDP types in order: 133 RS, 134 RA, 135 NS, 136 NA. Remember: 133-134 are routers, 135-136 are neighbours.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1441';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
  <div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: NDP Message Types</div><p>Each NDP message is an ICMPv6 packet with its own type number.</p><ul><li>133 = RS, Router Solicitation.</li><li>134 = RA, Router Advertisement.</li><li>135 = NS, Neighbor Solicitation.</li><li>136 = NA, Neighbor Advertisement.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option D</span> <strong>NA</strong>: NA, Neighbor Advertisement, is ICMPv6 Type 136.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>RS</strong>: RS is Type 133. It is the host asking for a router, not a Neighbor Advertisement.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>RA</strong>: RA is Type 134. It is the router announcing itself, not a Neighbor Advertisement.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>NS</strong>: NS is Type 135. It asks for a MAC address, not a Neighbor Advertisement.</li></ul></div>
<div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>NDP types in order: 133 RS, 134 RA, 135 NS, 136 NA. Remember 133 and 134 are about routers, 135 and 136 are about neighbours.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1442';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: NDP Message Types</div><p>Each NDP message is an ICMPv6 packet with a fixed type number.</p><ul><li>133 = RS, Router Solicitation. 134 = RA, Router Advertisement.</li><li>135 = NS, Neighbor Solicitation. 136 = NA, Neighbor Advertisement.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option D</span> <strong>136</strong>: NA, Neighbor Advertisement, is ICMPv6 Type 136. It sends the MAC address back to the requester, as unicast.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>135</strong>: 135 is NS, Neighbor Solicitation, the NDP version of an ARP request.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>133</strong>: 133 is RS, Router Solicitation, a host asking if any routers are on the link.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>134</strong>: 134 is RA, Router Advertisement, a router announcing itself and the network prefix.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>NDP types in order: 133 RS, 134 RA, 135 NS, 136 NA. Remember "133-134 routers, 135-136 neighbours".</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1443';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: NDP Message Types</div><p>Each NDP message is an ICMPv6 packet with a fixed type number. Do not mix up NS/NA (for MAC addresses) with RS/RA (for finding routers).</p><ul><li>133 = RS, Router Solicitation</li><li>134 = RA, Router Advertisement</li><li>135 = NS, Neighbor Solicitation</li><li>136 = NA, Neighbor Advertisement</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option D</span> <strong>RS</strong>: RS (Router Solicitation) is ICMPv6 Type 133. A host sends it to FF02::2 (all routers) to ask: are there any routers on this link?</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>NS</strong>: NS (Neighbor Solicitation) is Type 135, used to learn a MAC address.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>NA</strong>: NA (Neighbor Advertisement) is Type 136, the reply to an NS.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>RA</strong>: RA (Router Advertisement) is Type 134, sent by a router.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>NDP types in order: 133 RS, 134 RA, 135 NS, 136 NA. Remember 133-134 routers, 135-136 neighbours.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1444';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: NDP Message Types</div><p>NDP messages are ICMPv6 packets, and each one has a fixed type number:</p><ul><li>133 RS, Router Solicitation.</li><li>134 RA, Router Advertisement.</li><li>135 NS, Neighbor Solicitation.</li><li>136 NA, Neighbor Advertisement.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option D</span> <strong>133</strong>: RS, Router Solicitation, is ICMPv6 type 133. A host sends it to FF02::2, all routers, to ask if any router is on the link.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>135</strong>: 135 is Neighbor Solicitation, NS, not Router Solicitation.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>134</strong>: 134 is Router Advertisement, RA, not Router Solicitation.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>136</strong>: 136 is Neighbor Advertisement, NA, not Router Solicitation.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>NDP types in order: 133 RS, 134 RA, 135 NS, 136 NA. Remember: 133-134 are routers, 135-136 are neighbours.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1445';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
  <div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: NDP Message Types</div><p>Each NDP message is an ICMPv6 packet with its own type number.</p><ul><li>133 = RS, Router Solicitation.</li><li>134 = RA, Router Advertisement.</li><li>135 = NS, Neighbor Solicitation.</li><li>136 = NA, Neighbor Advertisement.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option A</span> <strong>RA</strong>: RA, Router Advertisement, is ICMPv6 Type 134.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>NA</strong>: NA is Type 136. It is the reply that gives a MAC address, not a Router Advertisement.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>NS</strong>: NS is Type 135. It asks for a MAC address, not a Router Advertisement.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>RS</strong>: RS is Type 133. It is the host asking for a router, not a Router Advertisement.</li></ul></div>
<div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>NDP types in order: 133 RS, 134 RA, 135 NS, 136 NA. Remember 133 and 134 are about routers, 135 and 136 are about neighbours.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1446';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: NDP Message Types</div><p>Each NDP message is an ICMPv6 packet with a fixed type number.</p><ul><li>133 = RS, Router Solicitation. 134 = RA, Router Advertisement.</li><li>135 = NS, Neighbor Solicitation. 136 = NA, Neighbor Advertisement.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option A</span> <strong>134</strong>: RA, Router Advertisement, is ICMPv6 Type 134. It is sent by a router to all nodes, to announce the network prefix.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>133</strong>: 133 is RS, Router Solicitation, a host asking if any routers are on the link.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>136</strong>: 136 is NA, Neighbor Advertisement, the reply that sends a MAC address back.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>135</strong>: 135 is NS, Neighbor Solicitation, the NDP version of an ARP request.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>NDP types in order: 133 RS, 134 RA, 135 NS, 136 NA. Remember "133-134 routers, 135-136 neighbours".</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1447';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: NDP Address Resolution</div><p>To learn a neighbour''s MAC address, a host sends an NS message. An NS is the NDP version of an ARP request: Hi, what is your MAC address?</p><p>It is sent to that neighbour''s solicited-node multicast address. Only the node whose address matches that group answers. That is much more efficient than ARP, because an ARP request is a broadcast to ALL hosts.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option C</span> <strong>The neighbor''s solicited-node multicast address</strong>: An NS is sent to the neighbour''s solicited-node multicast address (FF02::1:FF + last 24 bits of its address).</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>The all-nodes multicast address (FF02::1)</strong>: That reaches every node on the link. An NS targets only the one neighbour''s solicited-node group.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>The all-routers multicast address (FF02::2)</strong>: That reaches routers only. An NS for address resolution goes to the neighbour''s solicited-node group.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>The unicast address of the neighbor</strong>: The host does not know the neighbour''s MAC yet, so it cannot unicast to it. It multicasts to the solicited-node group instead.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>NS for address resolution always goes to the neighbour''s solicited-node multicast address.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1448';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Neighbor Table</div><p>IPv6 does not use ARP, so there is no ARP table. Instead, NDP builds an IPv6 neighbor table. It maps each neighbour’s IPv6 address to its MAC address. The table also shows the state of each neighbour, and REACH means the neighbour is reachable.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option A</span> <strong>show ipv6 neighbor</strong>: show ipv6 neighbor displays the IPv6 neighbor table.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>show ipv6 route</strong>: That shows the routing table, not the neighbour table.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>show ipv6 interface</strong>: That shows interface addresses and status, not the neighbour table.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>show arp</strong>: That shows the IPv4 ARP table, not the IPv6 neighbor table.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>IPv6 has no ARP table. The command is show ipv6 neighbor.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1449';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
  <div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: NDP Router Discovery</div><p>A host that needs a router sends a Router Solicitation to the all-routers multicast address.</p><p>Every router on the link hears it and can reply with a Router Advertisement.</p></div>
  <div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option B</span> <strong>FF02::2</strong>: RS messages are sent to FF02::2, the all-routers multicast address.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>FF02::5</strong>: That is the all-OSPF-routers address, not the all-routers address.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>FF02::1</strong>: That is the all-nodes address. An RS is for routers, not all nodes.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>FF02::9</strong>: That is the all-RIP-routers address, not the all-routers address.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>RS goes to FF02::2 (all routers). The RA comes back to FF02::1 (all nodes).</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1450';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: NDP Router Discovery</div><p>A router sends Router Advertisements to the all-nodes multicast address.</p><ul><li>FF02::1 reaches every host on the link in one packet.</li><li>That is how hosts get the prefix and the SLAAC information.</li><li>It is the other way round for RS: a host sends a Router Solicitation to FF02::2, all routers.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option C</span> <strong>FF02::1</strong>: RA messages go to FF02::1, the all-nodes multicast address.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>FF02::6</strong>: That is the all-OSPF-DR/BDR address, not the all-nodes address.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>FF02::A</strong>: That is the all-EIGRP-routers address, not the all-nodes address.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>FF02::2</strong>: That is the all-routers address. An RA goes to all nodes, not to routers.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>RS goes to FF02::2 (all routers). RA comes back to FF02::1 (all nodes).</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1451';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: SLAAC</div><p>SLAAC stands for Stateless Address Auto-configuration. It lets a host automatically make its own IPv6 address, with no server involved.</p><p>How it works: the host sends an RS, the router replies with an RA. The RA tells the host the IPv6 prefix of the local link. The host then uses that prefix to make its own address, and makes the interface ID with EUI-64 or a random value.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option B</span> <strong>Stateless Address Auto-configuration</strong>: SLAAC stands for Stateless Address Auto-configuration.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>Stateful Address Allocation Configuration</strong>: SLAAC is stateless, and the expansion is Address Auto-configuration.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>Stateful Address Auto-configuration</strong>: SLAAC is stateless: no server tracks which address was given out.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>Stateless Address Assignment Control</strong>: The word is Auto-configuration, not Assignment Control.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>SLAAC = Stateless Address Auto-configuration. Stateless is the key word: no server keeps state.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1452';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: SLAAC</div><p>SLAAC lets a host make its own IPv6 address. The host sends an RS to find a router. The router answers with an RA that carries the prefix of the local link. The host then takes that prefix and adds its own interface ID.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option D</span> <strong>RS/RA</strong>: The RS/RA pair lets the host learn the prefix it needs for SLAAC.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>RA only</strong>: The RA carries the prefix, but the host must first ask for it with an RS.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>NS/NA</strong>: NS/NA are for address resolution and DAD, not for learning a prefix.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>NS only</strong>: NS is used to resolve a neighbour’s MAC address, not to learn a prefix.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>SLAAC prefix learning = RS asks, RA answers. NS/NA are for neighbours, not prefixes.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1453';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
  <div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: SLAAC Interface ID</div><p>With SLAAC, the host builds its own address.</p><p>It learns the prefix from the router''s RA message.</p><p>Then it makes the interface ID itself.</p></div>
  <div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option B</span> <strong>EUI-64, or randomly</strong>: The interface ID is made with EUI-64, or it is randomly generated.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>Randomly only, never EUI-64</strong>: Both methods are valid, EUI-64 or random.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>From the router''s MAC address only</strong>: The ID comes from the host''s own MAC (EUI-64) or is random, not from the router.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>From the DHCPv6 server</strong>: SLAAC needs no DHCP server. The host makes the interface ID itself.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>SLAAC interface ID = EUI-64 or random. No server is involved.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1454';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: Duplicate Address Detection (DAD)</div><p>DAD stands for Duplicate Address Detection. It is one of the jobs of NDP.</p><ul><li>Before a host starts using a new IPv6 address, DAD checks that no other device on the link is already using it.</li><li>It runs any time an interface starts up or an address is configured on it.</li><li>How it works: the host sends an NS to its own solicited-node multicast address. If no reply comes back, the address is safe to use. If an NA comes back, another host is already using it.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option B</span> <strong>Duplicate Address Detection</strong>: DAD stands for Duplicate Address Detection. It checks an address is free before the host uses it.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>Dynamic Address Detection</strong>: The word is Duplicate, not Dynamic.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>Dynamic Address Discovery</strong>: Neither word is right. It is Duplicate Address Detection.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>Duplicate Address Distribution</strong>: The word is Detection, not Distribution.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>DAD = Duplicate Address Detection. No reply to the NS means the address is free. A reply means it is a duplicate.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1455';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: Duplicate Address Detection (DAD)</div><p>DAD is another job of NDP. It lets a host check if another device on the local link is already using the same IPv6 address.</p><p>How it works: the host sends an NS to its OWN solicited-node multicast address. If NO reply comes back, the address is unique and safe to use. If an NA reply comes back, another host is already using that address, so it is a duplicate.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option A</span> <strong>Duplicate Address Detection (DAD)</strong>: Duplicate Address Detection (DAD) is the mechanism that checks for an address clash on the local link.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>Neighbor Discovery Protocol</strong>: NDP is the overall protocol. DAD is the specific check it performs.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>Router Advertisement</strong>: An RA carries the prefix. It does not check for duplicate addresses.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>Stateless Address Auto-configuration</strong>: SLAAC is how the address is created. DAD is the check that it is not already in use.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>DAD is the check that asks: is anyone already using this address? It runs before an address goes live.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1456';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: Duplicate Address Detection (DAD)</div><p>DAD checks if another device on the local link is already using the same IPv6 address. The host sends an NS to its own solicited-node multicast address. If no reply comes back, the address is safe to use. If an NA reply comes back, another host is already using it.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option B</span> <strong>NS/NA</strong>: DAD uses NS and NA messages. No new message types to learn.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>RA only</strong>: An RA carries a prefix. DAD needs an NS and watches for an NA reply.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>RS only</strong>: An RS asks for a router. DAD needs an NS and watches for an NA reply.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>RS/RA</strong>: RS/RA are for router discovery and prefix learning, not for DAD.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>DAD = NS asks, NA answers. RS/RA are for routers, NS/NA are for neighbours and DAD.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1457';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
  <div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: Duplicate Address Detection (DAD)</div><p>DAD checks whether another device on the link is already using an address.</p><p>The host sends an NS for the new address to that address''s own solicited-node multicast group.</p><p>If no reply comes back, the address is free. If an NA comes back, the address is already in use.</p></div>
  <div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option B</span> <strong>Its own solicited-node multicast address</strong>: For DAD the NS is sent to the new address''s own solicited-node multicast address.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>The all-nodes multicast address (FF02::1)</strong>: DAD targets only the solicited-node group of the address being checked, not all nodes.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>Its own link-local unicast address</strong>: The NS is multicast to the solicited-node group, not sent unicast to itself.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>The all-routers multicast address (FF02::2)</strong>: DAD is a host check, not a router query. It goes to the solicited-node group, not to all routers.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>DAD NS goes to the address''s OWN solicited-node group. That is what makes the check work.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1458';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Link-Local Addresses and the Routing Table</div><p>Link-local addresses work on one link only. Routers will not forward packets sent to a link-local destination.</p><ul><li>Because of that, no route for a link-local address is added to the routing table.</li><li>Connected routes and local host routes are added for the prefixes assigned to interfaces, but link-local routes are not.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option C</span> <strong>No</strong>: No. Link-local addresses are valid only on the local link, so no route for them is added to the routing table.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>Yes, as connected routes</strong>: Connected routes are for the prefixes assigned to interfaces, not for link-local addresses.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>Only on Ethernet interfaces</strong>: Link-local routes are not added on any interface type.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>Yes</strong>: Link-local addresses never appear as routes in the table.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Link-local = local link only. No routing table entry, on any interface.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1459';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Static Routes</div><p>There are three kinds of static route, the same for IPv4 and IPv6:</p><ul><li>Directly attached: only the exit interface is given</li><li>Recursive: only the next-hop address is given</li><li>Fully specified: BOTH the exit interface and the next hop are given</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option C</span> <strong>exit interface (only)</strong>: In a directly attached static route only the exit interface is specified. The router forwards the packet out of the named interface and resolves the next hop itself.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>exit interface and next hop</strong>: That is the fully specified form, not the directly attached form.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>the administrative distance</strong>: Administrative distance is a separate, optional value, not what is specified instead of the interface.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>next hop (only)</strong>: That is the recursive form, not the directly attached form.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><ul><li>Directly attached = exit interface only.</li><li>Recursive = next hop only.</li><li>Fully specified = both.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1460';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Static Routes</div><p>A static route is made with ipv6 route, then the destination and prefix length, then the exit interface, the next hop, or both. The three forms are:</p><ul><li>Directly attached: exit interface only.</li><li>Recursive: next hop only.</li><li>Fully specified: both.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option B</span> <strong>directly attached</strong>: Directly attached means only the exit interface is given. That is the form described here.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>recursive</strong>: Recursive gives only the next hop address.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>floating</strong>: Floating is not a route form. It is a backup route with a higher administrative distance. It is only used if the main route goes away.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>fully specified</strong>: Fully specified gives both the exit interface and the next hop.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Static route forms: directly attached = exit interface only, recursive = next hop only, fully specified = both.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1461';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
  <div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Static Routes</div><p>An IPv6 static route can name the exit interface, the next hop, or both.</p><ul><li>Directly attached: exit interface only.</li><li>Recursive: next hop only.</li><li>Fully specified: both.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option D</span> <strong>recursive</strong>: Recursive is the form where only the next hop is given. The router must then look up the routing table a second time to find which interface leads to that next hop.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>floating</strong>: A floating static route is a backup route with a higher administrative distance. It is only used if the main route goes away.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>fully specified</strong>: The fully specified form gives both the exit interface and the next hop.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>directly attached</strong>: The directly attached form gives only the exit interface.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Directly attached = exit interface only, recursive = next hop only, fully specified = both.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1462';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Static Routes</div><p>There are three kinds of static route, and they work the same in IPv4 and IPv6.</p><ul><li>Directly attached: exit interface only.</li><li>Recursive: next hop only.</li><li>Fully specified: both the exit interface and the next hop.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option C</span> <strong>next hop (only)</strong>: In a recursive static route only the next hop is given. The router looks the next hop up a second time to find which interface reaches it, which is why it is called recursive.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>the outgoing interface and the next hop</strong>: That is the fully specified form, not the recursive form.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>exit interface (only)</strong>: That is the directly attached form, not the recursive form.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>exit interface and next hop</strong>: That is the fully specified form, not the recursive form.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Recursive = next hop only. The router looks up the next hop a second time to find the exit interface.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1463';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Static Routes</div><p>There are three kinds of static route, the same for IPv4 and IPv6:</p><ul><li>Directly attached: only the exit interface is given</li><li>Recursive: only the next-hop address is given</li><li>Fully specified: BOTH the exit interface and the next hop are given</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option A</span> <strong>fully specified</strong>: The fully specified form is the one described here. Both the exit interface and the next hop are given in the command.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>directly attached</strong>: Directly attached means exit interface only, with no next hop.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>recursive</strong>: Recursive means next hop only, with no exit interface.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>floating</strong>: A floating static route is a backup route with a higher administrative distance. It is not a form defined by what is specified in the command.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Static route forms: directly attached = exit interface only, recursive = next hop only, fully specified = both.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1464';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Static Route Configuration</div><p>A directly attached static route names the exit interface only: ipv6 route destination/prefix-length exit-interface. The router forwards out of that interface. Note: in IPv6 this form does not work on Ethernet interfaces. It only works on serial interfaces. In IPv4 it works on Ethernet too.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option C) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option C</span> <strong>ipv6 route destination/prefix-length exit-interface</strong>: The directly attached form is: ipv6 route destination/prefix-length exit-interface.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>ipv6 route destination/prefix-length next-hop</strong>: That is the recursive form, not the directly attached form.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>ip route destination mask exit-interface</strong>: That is the IPv4 command format, not the IPv6 one.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>ipv6 route destination/prefix-length exit-interface next-hop</strong>: That is the fully specified form, not the directly attached form.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Directly attached = exit interface only. Watch the missing next-hop in the command.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1465';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
  <div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Static Route Configuration</div><p>A recursive static route names only the next hop.</p><p>It is called recursive because the router looks up the routing table twice.</p><p>First it finds the next hop, then it finds which interface leads to that next hop.</p></div>
  <div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option D) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option D</span> <strong>ipv6 route destination/prefix-length next-hop</strong>: The recursive form is ipv6 route destination/prefix-length next-hop.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>ipv6 route destination/prefix-length exit-interface next-hop</strong>: That is the fully specified form, not the recursive form.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>ipv6 route destination/prefix-length exit-interface</strong>: That is the directly attached form, not the recursive form.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>ip route destination mask next-hop</strong>: That is the IPv4 command format, not the IPv6 one.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Recursive = next hop only. Watch for the missing exit interface in the command.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1466';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Static Route Configuration</div><p>A fully specified static route names both the exit interface and the next hop.</p><ul><li>Command: ipv6 route destination/prefix-length exit-interface next-hop.</li><li>This is the form you must use when the next hop is a link-local address. The router cannot work out on its own which interface a link-local address is on.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option B</span> <strong>ipv6 route destination/prefix-length exit-interface next-hop</strong>: That is the fully specified form. It gives both the exit interface and the next hop.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>ipv6 route destination/prefix-length next-hop</strong>: That is the recursive form. It is missing the exit interface.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>ipv6 route destination/prefix-length</strong>: That command is incomplete. A fully specified route needs both the exit interface and the next hop.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>ipv6 route destination/prefix-length exit-interface</strong>: That is the directly attached form. It is missing the next hop.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Fully specified = BOTH exit interface and next hop. It is the only form that works with a link-local next hop.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1467';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content"><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Static Routes on Ethernet</div><p>A directly attached static route names only the exit interface. On a multi-access Ethernet interface that does not work: the router has no next-hop address to resolve, so the command is accepted but the route is not installed.</p><p>Directly attached routes only work on serial interfaces. In IPv4 they DO work on Ethernet, but in IPv6 they do not.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option B) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option B</span> <strong>directly attached (the command will work, but the route will not)</strong>: A directly attached static route cannot be used on an Ethernet interface: the command is accepted, but the route is not installed.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option A</span> <strong>recursive</strong>: A recursive route works on Ethernet because it names a next-hop address.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>fully specified</strong>: A fully specified route works on Ethernet because it names both the interface and the next hop.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>floating</strong>: A floating static route is a backup route with a higher administrative distance. It is not the form that fails on Ethernet.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Ethernet needs a next-hop address. Directly attached has none, so the route never installs.</p></div><div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div></div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1468';
UPDATE questions SET explanation = '<div class="explanation-rich-content">
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Core Concept: IPv6 Static Routes with a Link-Local Next Hop</div><p>A link-local address is only valid on one link, so the router cannot work out which interface it is on from the next hop alone. The route must name both the exit interface and the link-local next hop. That is the fully specified form.</p></div><div class="expl-card correct-card"><h4 class="section-title-correct">✓ Why the Correct Answer (Option A) is Correct:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag correct">Option A</span> <strong>fully specified</strong>: With a link-local next hop you must use a fully specified static route, exit interface plus next hop.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card incorrect-card"><h4 class="section-title-incorrect">✗ Why Other Options are Incorrect:</h4><ul><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option B</span> <strong>directly attached</strong>: That names only the exit interface and has no next hop at all.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option C</span> <strong>recursive</strong>: That names only the next hop. The router cannot resolve a link-local next hop to an interface on its own.</li><li><span class="opt-tag wrong">Option D</span> <strong>floating</strong>: That is a backup route with a higher administrative distance, not a route form for link-local next hops.</li></ul></div><div class="expl-card tip-card"><h4 class="section-title-tip">💡 CCNA Exam Tip:</h4><p>Link-local next hop = fully specified, always. The router needs the interface AND the address.</p></div>
<div class="expl-card concept-card"><div class="section-title-concept">📌 Source: Anki Flashcards - Network Fundamentals (CCNA / Day 33 Flashcards - IPv6 (Part 3)</div></div>
</div>' WHERE question_no = 'Anki-NF-1469';
